   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network carries a video stream from the internet to a laptop through a series of steps involving routers, access points, radio channels, and packet transmission. Here\u2019s a simplified explanation:\n\n1. **Router and Access Point**: The router is the central hub of your Wi-Fi network. It connects to the internet and broadcasts a wireless signal that other devices can receive. An access point is a type of router that is specifically designed to provide a wireless network for a single device, like a laptop.\n\n2. **Wireless Signal**: The router broadcasts a wireless signal that covers a certain area, typically a few hundred feet. This signal is invisible to the naked eye but can be detected by devices that are within its range.\n\n3. **Laptop and Devices**: The laptop and other devices within the range of the router or access point receive the wireless signal. The laptop, in particular, is equipped with a Wi-Fi card that can connect to the network.\n\n4. **Packet Transmission**: When the laptop wants to send data, it breaks the data into smaller units called packets. These packets are then sent through the wireless signal to the router or access point.\n\n5. **Router/Access Point**: The router or access point receives these packets and forwards them to the internet."
